WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.040
In the first film, a rod reported light by driving cGMP down from baseline.

2
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.500
Why does that exact same molecule rise to relax an artery, and how do drugs alter

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.320
it? A classic vessel experiment pointed the way.

4
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:24.573
Mount a ring of rabbit artery in warm oxygenated salt solution and add

5
00:00:24.573 --> 00:00:31.153
acetylcholine. When the thin inner lining, the endothelium, stays intact, the artery

6
00:00:31.153 --> 00:00:32.913
ring relaxes and widens.

7
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:38.293
Rub that lining off, and acetylcholine no longer relaxes the ring.

8
00:00:39.473 --> 00:00:42.033
The lining must pass a signal to the muscle beneath.

9
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:48.853
On an endothelial cell, acetylcholine binds its receptor and raises its calcium.

10
00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:55.493
Calcium switches on nitric oxide synthase, which makes nitric oxide gas from

11
00:00:55.493 --> 00:01:00.913
arginine. Small and uncharged, this gas needs no vesicles or channels.

12
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:07.333
It diffuses freely across both membranes and reacts away within seconds, reaching

13
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.333
only near neighbors.

14
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:15.307
In smooth muscle, nitric oxide binds the heme iron of soluble guanylyl cyclase.

15
00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:20.087
The enzyme switches on, and cGMP climbs sharply.

16
00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:25.607
That rise activates protein kinase G, which works against contraction.

17
00:01:26.927 --> 00:01:29.567
The muscle relaxes, and the artery widens.

18
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.780
But nitric oxide does not last.

19
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.860
Once it stops arriving, the cyclase falls quiet.

20
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.860
Think back to the rod, where an enzyme destroyed this same messenger.

21
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.820
What could bring cGMP back down, so the muscle tightens again?

22
00:01:52.783 --> 00:01:55.483
A phosphodiesterase, just as in the rod.

23
00:01:56.783 --> 00:02:02.103
Here it is called PDE5, and it breaks cGMP into inactive GMP.

24
00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:07.303
Relaxation lasts only while synthesis keeps up with this breakdown.

25
00:02:08.663 --> 00:02:11.203
When synthesis stops, the muscle tightens again.

26
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:16.097
Two familiar drugs act on opposite sides of this balance.

27
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:22.637
Nitroglycerin, given for chest pain since the nineteenth century, is converted in

28
00:02:22.637 --> 00:02:24.297
tissue into nitric oxide.

29
00:02:25.577 --> 00:02:29.117
It drives synthesis up even without endothelial signals.

30
00:02:30.537 --> 00:02:34.537
Sildenafil sits inside the catalytic pocket of PDE5.

31
00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:40.137
By slowing hydrolysis, it lets each burst of the messenger last longer.

32
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:45.850
Now place the rod from the first film beside this smooth muscle cell.

33
00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:53.750
Both use teal cGMP and a rose phosphodiesterase, yet wiring reverses the enzyme's

34
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.490
role. In the rod, its breakdown enzyme creates the light signal.

35
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.050
In the artery, breakdown ends relaxation.

36
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:09.703
Because arteries signal by raising cGMP, slowing breakdown strengthens the response

37
00:03:09.703 --> 00:03:11.643
to every burst of nitric oxide.

38
00:03:13.023 --> 00:03:15.943
Same molecule, opposite direction, different meaning.

39
00:03:17.223 --> 00:03:18.903
Keep this rule alongside the first.

40
00:03:20.203 --> 00:03:25.263
When rising cGMP carries the signal, blocking breakdown amplifies it.
